    "phrases": [
      "November 9, 1989. East Berlin.",
      "A government",
      "official is answering",
      "questions on live television.",
      "Near the end,",
      "a journalist asks when a new",
      "travel rule will take effect.",
      "The official looks",
      "through his papers.",
      "Immediately. Without delay.",
      "A few hours later, thousands",
      "of people are standing",
      "at the Berlin Wall, demanding",
      "to cross into the West.",
      "But there is a problem.",
      "Nobody has ordered the border",
      "guards to open the gates.",
      "And the people",
      "at the front have no intention",
      "of going home.",
      "In 1961, East Germany sealed",
      "the border dividing Berlin.",
      "Families found themselves",
      "separated by concrete, fences,",
      "and armed checkpoints.",
      "West Berlin remained",
      "an island surrounded",
      "by East German territory.",
      "For ordinary East Germans,",
      "visiting the other",
      "side could be impossible.",
      "People had tried to escape",
      "over the Wall, through tunnels,",
      "and across the border.",
      "Some made it.",
      "Others were arrested.",
      "Some were killed.",
      "For nearly three decades,",
      "the government controlled",
      "who could leave, and when.",
      "Then, in 1989,",
      "that control began to collapse.",
      "East Germans were finding",
      "routes to the West",
      "through other countries.",
      "Hungary opened",
      "a path toward Austria.",
      "Thousands more sought",
      "refuge at the West German",
      "embassy in Prague.",
      "Even when officials",
      "stopped one route,",
      "people looked for another.",
      "Meanwhile, demonstrations",
      "spread across East Germany.",
      "In Leipzig,",
      "crowds filled the streets.",
      "On November 4,",
      "hundreds of thousands",
      "gathered in East Berlin,",
      "calling for change.",
      "The country's long-serving",
      "leader, Erich Honecker,",
      "had already been replaced.",
      "His successor,",
      "Egon Krenz, promised reforms.",
      "But the crowds",
      "wanted more than promises.",
      "And there was one",
      "demand the government",
      "could no longer ignore.",
      "People wanted",
      "to travel without asking",
      "the state for permission.",
      "On November 6,",
      "officials proposed new rules.",
      "East Germans would still need",
      "documents, applications,",
      "and approval.",
      "To people who had waited",
      "years to travel freely,",
      "it was not enough.",
      "Three days later,",
      "the leadership tried again.",
      "This time, a small",
      "group of officials drafted",
      "emergency travel regulations.",
      "The rules would make it much",
      "easier to apply for visits",
      "and permanent departure.",
      "Crucially, they were supposed",
      "to be announced the following",
      "morning, when the authorities",
      "could put procedures in place.",
      "The government was preparing",
      "a controlled change.",
      "It was about to get",
      "something very different.",
      "Late that afternoon, Krenz",
      "handed the new document",
      "to Günter Schabowski,",
      "a senior Communist Party",
      "official who was about to face",
      "the international press.",
      "Schabowski had not taken",
      "part in drafting the details.",
      "He had notes, a document,",
      "and a live television audience.",
      "At six in the evening,",
      "the press conference began.",
      "For almost an hour,",
      "he spoke about politics,",
      "party changes, and the future",
      "of East Germany.",
      "The most important",
      "announcement came",
      "near the end.",
      "An Italian journalist,",
      "Riccardo Ehrman,",
      "challenged him",
      "about the government's travel",
      "policy.",
      "Schabowski searched",
      "his papers and read",
      "out the new regulation.",
      "East Germans could now apply",
      "to travel abroad without having",
      "to prove a special reason.",
      "Permission would be granted",
      "quickly.",
      "Permanent departure",
      "would be possible",
      "through official crossing points.",
      "The words sounded significant,",
      "but also bureaucratic.",
      "You could apply.",
      "You could receive permission.",
      "The system would process",
      "your request.",
      "Then another journalist,",
      "Peter Brinkmann, asked",
      "the question that changed",
      "the evening.",
      "When did the rule take effect?",
      "Schabowski checked his papers.",
      "He didn't find a clear",
      "answer in his notes.",
      "So he answered,",
      "in German, Sofort.",
      "Unverzüglich. Immediately.",
      "Without delay.",
      "That was not how",
      "the government had planned",
      "to introduce the new rules.",
      "And thousands of people",
      "were about to take",
      "him at his word.",
      "Within minutes, news agencies",
      "began sending out alerts.",
      "Television reports condensed",
      "a complicated travel regulation",
      "into a much simpler message.",
      "East Germany had opened",
      "its border.",
      "The problem was that,",
      "at the actual border,",
      "almost nothing had changed.",
      "The gates were still closed.",
      "The guards",
      "were still checking documents.",
      "Their instructions",
      "had not suddenly been replaced",
      "by a new system.",
      "But people watching television",
      "saw a senior official say",
      "they could leave immediately.",
      "And they believed him.",
      "At first,",
      "only small groups appeared",
      "at Berlin's checkpoints.",
      "Guards told them",
      "to come back the next day.",
      "Then the eight o'clock news",
      "announced that the border",
      "was open.",
      "More people came.",
      "At Bornholmer Straße,",
      "one of the crossings",
      "into West Berlin, an officer",
      "named Harald Jäger was trying",
      "to work out what",
      "he was supposed to do.",
      "He had watched",
      "Schabowski's announcement",
      "on television.",
      "He called his superiors.",
      "They had no clear",
      "instructions that matched what",
      "the public had just heard.",
      "Outside, the crowd",
      "continued to grow.",
      "By around nine thirty, hundreds",
      "of people were gathered there.",
      "They weren't trying",
      "to sneak across.",
      "They were demanding",
      "the journey they believed",
      "their own government",
      "had just authorized.",
      "Jäger and his colleagues",
      "were trapped",
      "between two realities.",
      "On television,",
      "the border was open.",
      "At their checkpoint, it was not.",
      "Then the authorities",
      "tried a solution that made",
      "everything worse.",
      "They would let a small",
      "number of the most",
      "insistent people through,",
      "hoping to relieve the pressure.",
      "But for some of those people,",
      "officials stamped",
      "across the photographs",
      "on their identity cards.",
      "The mark",
      "invalidated the document.",
      "Under the plan,",
      "they would not be allowed",
      "to return to East Germany.",
      "And the people",
      "crossing did not know.",
      "Imagine thinking",
      "you are making a short visit",
      "across town, while the officials",
      "behind the counter are treating",
      "it as a permanent departure.",
      "This was meant",
      "to protect the border.",
      "Instead, it gave the crowd",
      "proof that crossing",
      "was possible.",
      "People who had been told",
      "to wait could see",
      "others being let through.",
      "They pushed",
      "for the same right.",
      "The pressure",
      "on the checkpoint increased.",
      "By ten o'clock,",
      "the problem was no longer how",
      "to process applications.",
      "It was how to hold",
      "back the people outside.",
      "Television kept",
      "spreading the news.",
      "At 10:42,",
      "a West German news broadcast",
      "declared that the gates",
      "in the Wall were wide open.",
      "At Bornholmer Straße,",
      "that still wasn't true.",
      "Not yet.",
      "The officers faced thousands",
      "of people and a barrier",
      "that was never designed",
      "to withstand a crowd this size.",
      "The possibility of panic",
      "or a violent",
      "confrontation was growing.",
      "Jäger contacted",
      "his superiors again.",
      "There was still no workable",
      "answer that would empty",
      "the checkpoint safely.",
      "And something",
      "else had changed.",
      "Many of the people",
      "outside did not want",
      "to abandon",
      "East Germany forever.",
      "They wanted to visit",
      "West Berlin, look around,",
      "and come home.",
      "The government's attempt",
      "to quietly turn",
      "a visit into permanent",
      "emigration had misunderstood",
      "the crowd completely.",
      "Soon, the guards",
      "had to confront a choice",
      "that no television announcement",
      "could make for them.",
      "Keep the gate closed,",
      "and risk the situation",
      "getting out of control.",
      "Or stop checking documents,",
      "and let everyone through.",
      "At about eleven thirty",
      "that night, Jäger and the other",
      "officers at Bornholmer Straße",
      "gave way to the crowd.",
      "The checks stopped.",
      "The barrier opened.",
      "People poured over the bridge",
      "into West Berlin.",
      "Some ran. Some walked.",
      "On the other side,",
      "strangers greeted them,",
      "cheered, and embraced them.",
      "People who had lived minutes",
      "apart, but behind different",
      "political systems for decades,",
      "could finally",
      "meet without a wall",
      "standing between them.",
      "Around twenty thousand people",
      "crossed at Bornholmer Straße",
      "in roughly the next",
      "forty-five minutes.",
      "Other Berlin crossings",
      "opened that night, too.",
      "What had begun",
      "as an announcement was now",
      "an irreversible public event.",
      "For months, East Germans",
      "had been leaving, protesting,",
      "and demanding change.",
      "The country's leadership",
      "had changed,",
      "and the government",
      "was struggling to maintain",
      "control over travel.",
      "Schabowski's announcement",
      "sent thousands to the border",
      "crossings before the new",
      "procedures were in place.",
      "As the crowds grew,",
      "the guards still had no clear",
      "instructions for handling them.",
      "At Bornholmer Straße,",
      "they finally made their own",
      "decision to let people cross.",
      "Schabowski had not intended",
      "to create that scene.",
      "He went home after the press",
      "conference, expecting",
      "the bureaucracy to carry",
      "out the regulation as planned.",
      "Instead, ordinary",
      "people had already made",
      "his words real.",
      "The concrete Wall",
      "did not disappear overnight.",
      "But its power to keep Berlin",
      "divided had been broken.",
      "Less than a year later,",
      "on October 3, 1990,",
      "East and West Germany",
      "were reunited.",
      "The world remembers the two",
      "words spoken at that press",
      "conference: Immediately.",
      "Without delay.",
      "But those words did not lift",
      "a single barrier.",
      "Thousands of people went",
      "to the gates and refused",
      "to leave.",
      "And the officers",
      "standing opposite them finally",
      "let them through.",
      "That evening,",
      "a journalist had asked",
      "when the border would open.",
      "By midnight, the people",
      "of Berlin had answered him."
    ],
